## Prerequisites<a id="prerequisites"></a>

- [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/), or [nox](https://nox.thea.codes/) with
  [antsibull-nox](https://docs.ansible.com/projects/antsibull-nox/) in one
  Python environment.
- [Podman](https://podman.io/) for the container-backed `ansible-test` sessions.
- An authorized API endpoint for the
  [Molecule scenario](./extensions/molecule/README.md).

## Checks<a id="checks"></a>

`runtime-check` runs by default. It installs a built archive in isolation and
retains only `meta/`, `plugins/`, `roles/` and `playbooks/`. It checks Python
imports, module documentation and role loading/syntax, then runs available
unit tests from a separate fixture directory against that installation.
Role deployment behavior remains covered by Molecule.

To check the exact archive retained for a release:

```sh
uv run noxfile.py -s runtime-check -- --artifact /path/to/collection.tar.gz
```

Run from the collection's Git root. If Nox is installed, use `nox`
instead of `uv run noxfile.py`.

```bash
uv run noxfile.py                 # fix, then run default checks
CI=true uv run noxfile.py         # check only
uv run noxfile.py -l              # list sessions
uv run noxfile.py -s lint         # Python, plugin YAML and typing
uv run noxfile.py -s helper-lint   # Python helpers
uv run noxfile.py -s ansible-test-sanity
uv run noxfile.py -s ansible-test-units
uv run noxfile.py -s ansible-test-integration
uv run noxfile.py -s molecule
```

`helper-lint` covers Python in `extensions/molecule/`, `roles/*/files/`,
`tools/`, `scripts/` and `tests/runtime_check.py`, using [ruff.toml](ruff.toml).
It runs by default; add it to CI configurations that list sessions explicitly.

Session definitions: [noxfile.py](noxfile.py).
Built-in session settings: [antsibull-nox.toml](antsibull-nox.toml).
Run container-backed sessions sequentially.

## Boundaries<a id="boundaries"></a>

Replace the [fixture](plugins/modules/fixture.py), its definition and tests
together. Bind the shared contract tests in
`tests/unit/plugins/modules/contract_subject.py`. For a family a definition
cannot express, copy `_fixture_nested.py`, its module and its tests. Edit hooks,
never the lifecycle sequence. The synthetic base and engine tests remain
independent of the product's fixtures.

The following files are skeleton-owned invariants. Keep them byte-identical
to the selected render; a required change goes through the skeleton:

- `plugins/module_utils/_lifecycle.py`, `_engine.py` and `_activation.py`.
- `extensions/molecule/shared/files/loss.py`, the one-response loss harness.
- `tests/unit/plugins/conftest.py`, the generic module runner.
- `test_contract.py`, `test_engine.py`, `test_activation.py` and
  `test_lifecycle.py` under `tests/unit/plugins/modules/`.

{# skeleton-variant: appliance-invariants #}
{# end skeleton-variant #}
The shared Molecule playbooks dispatch scenario hooks and retain their
skeleton-owned structure. `contract_subject.py` binds the contract tests to
the product; `_binding.py` binds the lifecycle to its client, error schema and
compatibility policy. Bring those bindings forward with product values kept.

`foundata.utils` owns byte transport, deadlines, JSON decoding and safe error
translation. The client, definitions, copied family utilities, guard rules,
modules, documentation fragments, fake product, scenario hooks and inventory
extractor are product-owned. The extractor writes
`tools/inventory/<series>.yml` beside itself. That inventory is development
evidence: it never ships and nothing reads it at runtime.

